<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Community News – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Community News</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Reporting From the School!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Describe others based on physical traits, personality, and type of relationship.<br>interactive: Exchange information on familiar topics with peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe community members featured in local news using physical traits and personality adjectives (e.g., 'The reporter has short black hair and is very friendly.' 'The artisan is tall, wears a Sombrero Pintao, and is very proud of his work.').<br>Can ask and answer questions about community events and traditions with peers (e.g., 'What did you like about the Carnival in Penonomé?' 'I liked the queen's beautiful outfit and the music.' 'Did you go to the school fair?' 'Yes, I went with my friends.').<br>Can exchange information with peers about popular landmarks in their community (e.g., 'Isla Colón is a popular landmark in our province.' 'Yes, I went last month and saw a lot of tourists.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a local school fair or a traditional parade (e.g., Desfile de las Mil Polleras or a Carnival parade float) and asks students to recall what they see or experienced. "Look at this picture! What do you see? Did you go to this fair/parade? What did you like?" Students share a few words or short sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to people in the picture (e.g., a Carnival Queen, an artisan, a reporter) and models descriptions using target grammar and vocabulary. "Look at her! She is the Carnival Queen. She has long, curly hair and a colorful Pollera. She is very proud. I remember I saw her last year during the parade." The teacher explicitly uses past simple verbs (saw, liked, went) and adjectives (colorful, proud, curly hair) and practices pronunciation of -ed endings, e.g., 'liked' /t/, 'celebrated' /ɪd/. Also models compound sentences with 'but': "I liked the music, but I didn't like the crowd."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new adjectives and past experiences.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'friendly' about how a person looks or how they act?" (Expected answer: "How they act.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I visited the market last week,' did I go yesterday or a long time ago?" (Expected answer: "A long time ago / last week.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Community News")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated dialogue between two friends, Carla and Luis, talking about a recent school fair and a local reporter who covered it. The dialogue includes descriptions of people and past events, using target vocabulary (reporter, community fair, artisan, traditional, friendly, proud, tall, short, curly hair, black straight hair) and grammar (past simple, compound sentences with 'but').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Exchanging information on familiar topics with peers and describing people and community members using physical traits and personality.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Whole Class):</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud twice. "What are Carla and Luis talking about? (School fair, reporter). Who did they see at the fair? (A reporter, an artisan)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Individual or Pairs):</b> Students receive sentences based on the dialogue. "The reporter has short black hair. (True/False)." "Carla liked the loud music at the fair. (True/False)." "The artisan was very proud of his Sombrero Pintao. (True/False)." Students circle T or F.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match the person to their description. E.g., "Reporter" -> "short black hair, friendly"; "Artisan" -> "tall, proud, Sombrero Pintao." This reinforces adjectives and nouns.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Describing people using physical traits and personality adjectives, sharing past experiences with the Past Simple tense, and using 'but' for contrasting ideas. Vocabulary includes: reporter, artisan, community fair, festival, parade, traditional, colorful, friendly, tall, short, curly hair, black straight hair, proud, market, banner. Pronunciation of past tense -ed endings and short/long vowel sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs with incomplete sentences from the dialogue or about other community events. "The reporter ____ (report) on the fair. He has ____ (short/long) hair and is very ____ (friendly/shy)." "I ____ (visit) the market, but I ____ (not buy) anything." They orally complete the sentences, focusing on correct past simple forms and adjective choices. Teacher monitors and provides immediate feedback on pronunciation (e.g., /t/ in 'liked', /d/ in 'moved', /ɪd/ in 'wanted') and grammar.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Picture Description Chains (Small Groups):</b> The teacher shows a series of pictures of different community members or local event participants (e.g., a cultural dancer, a chef at a school cafeteria, a local politician, a child holding a banner). In groups of 3-4, students take turns describing one person in a picture using at least two adjectives (physical and personality) and one past simple sentence about what they did or saw. For example: "This woman is tall and slender. She has black straight hair. She participated in the parade last year." The next student describes another person. Teacher reminds them to use 'but' if they want to contrast two ideas (e.g., "She was excited, but also a little tired.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Guess the Community Member" (Information Gap):</b> Students work in A/B pairs. Student A has a picture of a community member (e.g., a reporter, an artisan, a Carnival Queen) with some hidden information (e.g., their name, specific event they attended). Student B has a list of questions (Who is this? What did they do? Where did they go? What do they look like?). Student B asks questions, and Student A describes the person and their past actions using the target vocabulary and grammar, paying attention to pronunciation. Then they swap roles. Example: "What did the reporter do?" "He reported about the community fair." "What does he look like?" "He has short black hair and brown eyes. He is very friendly."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Community News")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play as "School News Reporters" conducting mini-interviews.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students work in groups of three. One student acts as a "School Reporter," and the other two act as "Community Members" (e.g., a student who participated in the school fair, a parent who helped decorate a mural, a teacher who organized a cultural event). The "Reporter" interviews the "Community Members" about their experiences at a recent school or community event (e.g., "The School Fair," "Community Day").
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reporters ask questions using "who, what, where, when, why, how" about the event and the people involved. E.g., "What did you do during the school fair?" "Who did you see there?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Community Members respond using the past simple tense, describing their actions and other people they saw, including physical traits and personality adjectives. E.g., "I prepared a banner for the fair." "I saw Mr. Carlos, the artisan. He is tall and friendly. He wore a Sombrero Pintao."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use compound sentences with 'but' to share preferences or express mild disagreement. E.g., "I liked the colorful floats, but I didn't like the loud music." "I loved the food, but I couldn't buy a souvenir."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reminds students to practice clear pronunciation, especially past tense -ed endings and short/long vowel sounds (e.g., banner vs. parade).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play, focusing on the use of target language and communicative effectiveness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "School News Reporters" role-play in their groups. The teacher circulates, listening and noting down examples of target language use and areas for improvement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially -ed endings and vowel sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of past simple tense.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of adjectives for physical appearance and personality.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of compound sentences with 'but'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation and exchange of information with peers.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Task completion (successfully interviewing and responding).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper before leaving.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to describe a community member by saying: _______."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask questions about past events like: _______."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of describing people or talking about past events was easy for you today? What was difficult?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use these new words and sentences to talk about your own community?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting speaking and interaction? (e.g., "The role-play activity was highly engaging, and students used many target adjectives.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., "Some students struggled with the '-ed' pronunciation; I need to dedicate a short warm-up activity to this in the next lesson. Also, encourage more complex sentences using 'but'.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about community members and past events. In Lesson 4, students will apply this knowledge to writing a short news report about a community event, integrating the vocabulary, grammar, and descriptive language practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>